-- baarah
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Baarah Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 18 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 8 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Baarah Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Baarah Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Baarah different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Baarah stands out for its historical significance and remote location in the northern Maldives. Unlike more developed islands, Baarah maintains its authentic character with a community still primarily engaged in traditional fishing and crafts. The island offers access to pristine northern marine life while providing a genuine cultural experience away from mass tourism."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Baarah Island?","answer":"As Baarah is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Baarah?","answer":"Baarah''s waters offer excellent marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip and whitetip), sea turtles, various ray species, and abundant reef fish. The deep waters nearby often attract pelagic species as well. The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'baarah';


-- bandidhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Bandidhoo Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 12 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 5 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Bandidhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Bandidhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year, making it suitable for water activities year-round."},{"question":"What makes Bandidhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Bandidhoo stands out for its excellent diving opportunities combined with authentic local culture. Unlike more developed islands, Bandidhoo maintains its traditional character while offering access to some of Dhaalu Atoll''s best marine environments. The island provides a genuine cultural experience away from mass tourism while still being accessible to travelers."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Bandidhoo Island?","answer":"As Bandidhoo is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities or at resort day visits."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Bandidhoo?","answer":"Bandidhoo''s waters offer excellent marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip and whitetip), sea turtles, various ray species, and abundant reef fish. The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'bandidhoo';


-- bileddhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Bileiydhoo Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 8-10 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 3-4 hours from Male). Most accommodations can assist with arranging transportation."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Bileiydhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Bileiydhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. This period is ideal for water activities, with excellent visibility for snorkeling and diving. The southwest monsoon (May to November) brings occasional rain showers but also lower prices and fewer tourists. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Bileiydhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Bileiydhoo stands out for its exceptional coral reefs and strong boat-building tradition. Unlike more developed islands, Bileiydhoo maintains its authentic character with a community still actively engaged in traditional crafts and fishing. The island offers access to Faafu Atoll''s spectacular marine life while providing a genuine cultural experience away from mass tourism. Its vibrant house reef, accessible directly from shore, makes it particularly appealing for snorkeling enthusiasts who can enjoy multiple sessions throughout the day without needing boat transportation."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Bileiydhoo Island?","answer":"As Bileiydhoo is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities or at resort day visits. Respecting local dress codes is an important way to show cultural sensitivity during your stay."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Bileiydhoo?","answer":"Bileiydhoo''s waters offer exceptional marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip and whitetip), sea turtles, various ray species, and abundant reef fish including parrotfish, angelfish, butterflyfish, and triggerfish. The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore, while boat excursions can take you to nearby thilas (underwater pinnacles) that attract even more diverse marine life. Faafu Atoll is known for its excellent coral health, making Bileiydhoo an ideal base for underwater exploration."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'bileddhoo';


