 featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Excursions","paragraphs":["Discover nearby dive sites with professional dive operators offering guided dives and certification courses."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Adventures","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands, pristine sandbanks, and discover the diverse beauty of Meemu Atoll."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience authentic Maldivian fishing techniques with local fishermen during sunset and night fishing trips."]},{"heading":"Cultural Immersion","paragraphs":["Learn about local traditions, participate in community activities, and experience authentic Maldivian island life."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy magical sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing."]},{"heading":"Community Life & Culture","paragraphs":["Mulah is home to approximately 700 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing sustainable tourism. The island''s community is known for their exceptional friendliness, hospitality, and peaceful way of life that creates a truly relaxing atmosphere for visitors."]},{"heading":"Island Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Mulah follows traditional patterns centered around fishing, family, and community activities. The island maintains a serene atmosphere where visitors can experience authentic Maldivian culture and customs in their most natural, peaceful setting."]},{"heading":"Local Traditions","paragraphs":["The community preserves traditional Maldivian practices including traditional music, dance, handicrafts, and fishing techniques. Visitors can observe and often participate in cultural activities and celebrations in this tranquil island environment."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Tourism","paragraphs":["Mulah has embraced sustainable tourism practices that benefit the local community while preserving the island''s natural environment and peaceful atmosphere for future generations to experience and enjoy."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Mulah Island?","answer":"Regular ferry services are also available from Male and nearby islands in Meemu Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Mulah?","answer":"The best time to visit Mulah is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable for beach activities and water sports. However, the island can be visited year-round with the wet season offering fewer crowds and a more peaceful atmosphere."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Mulah?","answer":"Mulah offers various budget-friendly accommodations including family guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages in a peaceful setting."},{"question":"What makes Mulah different from other islands?","answer":"Mulah is known for its exceptionally peaceful atmosphere and tranquil environment. The island offers a more serene experience compared to busier destinations, making it perfect for travelers seeking relaxation, authentic culture, and pristine natural beauty."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["kolhufushi","muli","maduvvaree","raimmandhoo","dhiggaru","naalaafushi","veyvah"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'boli-mulah';


-- burunee
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Thaa Atoll, Central Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 900 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.1 km long, 0.7 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"198 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Seaplane or domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Enchanting culture, pristine beaches, excellent snorkeling"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Culture enthusiasts, beach lovers, snorkelers, tranquility seekers"}],"island_overview":["Burunee Island is an enchanting local island in Thaa Atoll that offers visitors a captivating glimpse into traditional Maldivian life. This magical island combines natural beauty with a spellbinding atmosphere, providing an ideal destination for travelers seeking mystical cultural experiences and pristine tropical surroundings.","Known for its beautiful beaches, enchanting coral reefs, and excellent marine biodiversity, Burunee provides wonderful opportunities for snorkeling, swimming, and underwater exploration. The island''s captivating community embraces visitors with traditional Maldivian warmth and shares their magical way of life with enchanting hospitality.","What makes Burunee special is its enchanting natural environment and captivating island setting. Visitors can enjoy pristine beaches, explore rich marine ecosystems, and experience magical island culture while staying in comfortable accommodations that showcase the enchanting Maldivian lifestyle."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Burunee Island is beautifully located in the central part of Thaa Atoll, approximately 198 kilometers from Male. This location provides an enchanting and captivating experience while maintaining good accessibility to the capital and international airport through various transfer options.","The island''s position offers excellent protection from strong currents while providing access to pristine coral reefs and diverse marine ecosystems. Its location in the atoll makes it an ideal base for exploring the natural beauty and cultural richness of Thaa Atoll in its most enchanting form."]},{"heading":"Burunee: Enchanting Paradise & Marine Magic","paragraphs":["Burunee Island is blessed with exceptional natural beauty, featuring pristine white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and one of the most enchanting marine environments in Thaa Atoll. The island offers perfect conditions for beach relaxation and marine exploration in a captivating, magical setting."]},{"heading":"Pristine White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Burunee features enchanting white sand beaches with gentle slopes and calm waters, perfect for swimming, sunbathing, and magical beach activities."]},{"heading":"Enchanting House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef is easily accessible from the beach and offers captivating snorkeling with diverse marine life and colorful coral formations."]},{"heading":"Captivating Island Community","paragraphs":["Experience the magical spirit of traditional island life with friendly locals and enchanting Maldivian culture in a captivating community setting."]},{"heading":"Magical Sandbank Adventures","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks and pristine islands around Burunee for enchanting tropical paradise experiences with rich marine life."]},{"heading":"Beach Features","paragraphs":["Burunee''s coastal highlights include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Burunee feature:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Burunee offers a perfect blend of marine adventures, cultural experiences, and enchanting activities. The island''s captivating environment and magical atmosphere provide ideal conditions for various activities in a pristine, spellbinding setting."]},{"heading":"Enchanting House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the captivating house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility in enchanting waters."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Excursions","paragraphs":["Discover nearby pristine dive sites with professional dive operators offering guided dives and certification courses in enchanting marine environments."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Adventures","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands, pristine sandbanks, and discover the natural beauty of Thaa Atoll with captivating exploration opportunities."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience authentic Maldivian fishing techniques with local fishermen during sunset and night fishing trips in enchanting community settings."]},{"heading":"Magical Cultural Immersion","paragraphs":["Learn about local traditions, participate in community activities, and experience enchanting Maldivian island life with captivating cultural exchanges."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy pristine sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing in enchanting waters."]},{"heading":"Community Life & Culture","paragraphs":["Burunee is home to approximately 900 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing sustainable tourism. The island''s community is known for their exceptional friendliness, hospitality, and enchanting cultural traditions that create a captivating atmosphere for visitors."]},{"heading":"Island Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Burunee follows traditional patterns centered around fishing, family, and community activities. The island maintains an enchanting atmosphere where visitors can experience captivating Maldivian culture and customs in their most magical, spellbinding setting."]},{"heading":"Local Traditions","paragraphs":["The community preserves traditional Maldivian practices including traditional music, dance, handicrafts, and fishing techniques. Visitors can observe and often participate in cultural activities and celebrations in this enchanting island environment."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Tourism","paragraphs":["Burunee has embraced sustainable tourism practices that benefit the local community while preserving the island''s natural environment and enchanting cultural heritage for future generations to experience and enjoy."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Burunee Island?","answer":"Regular ferry services are also available from Male and nearby islands in Thaa Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Burunee?","answer":"The best time to visit Burunee is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable for beach activities and water sports. However, the island can be visited year-round with the wet season offering fewer crowds and a more enchanting local atmosphere."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Burunee?","answer":"Burunee offers various accommodations including enchanting guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages in a captivating setting."},{"question":"What makes Burunee''s atmosphere special?","answer":"Burunee is known for its enchanting and captivating natural environment. The island maintains traditional Maldivian cultural practices while offering a magical atmosphere perfect for tranquil exploration. Visitors can experience spellbinding cultural exchanges and participate in enchanting community activities."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["guraidhoo","gaadhiffushi","dhiyamigili","vilufushi","veymandoo","vandhoo","thimarafushi","omadhoo","madifushi","kinbidhoo","kandoodhoo","hirilandhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'burunee';


-- dhaandhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhaandhoo Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 17 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 5.5 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhaandhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhaandhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. For whale shark encounters, visit during the southwest monsoon (May to December). Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Dhaandhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Dhaandhoo stands out for its exceptional preservation of traditional Maldivian life in a remote setting. Unlike more developed islands, Dhaandhoo maintains its authentic character with a community still primarily engaged in traditional fishing. The island offers access to Gaafu Alifu Atoll''s spectacular marine life while providing a genuine cultural experience away from mass tourism."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Dhaandhoo Island?","answer":"As Dhaandhoo is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities or at resort day visits."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Dhaandhoo?","answer":"Dhaandhoo''s waters offer excellent marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip and whitetip), sea turtles, various ray species, and abundant reef fish. Seasonal visitors include whale sharks (May-December). The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhaandhoo';


-- dhanbidhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Laamu Atoll, Southern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Traditional Fishing Village"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 800 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.2 km long, 0.6 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"240 km south"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Domestic flight + speedboat, seaplane"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Traditional fishing, authentic culture, pristine beaches"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Cultural experiences, authentic island life, peaceful retreats"}],"island_overview":["Nestled in the pristine waters of Laamu Atoll, Dhanbidhoo Island represents the authentic heart of traditional Maldivian island life. This charming fishing village offers visitors a rare glimpse into the genuine culture and heritage that has shaped the Maldives for centuries, where time moves at the gentle pace of ocean tides and community bonds remain strong.","What makes Dhanbidhoo truly special is its commitment to preserving traditional ways of life while welcoming visitors with the legendary Maldivian hospitality. The island''s fishing community continues age-old practices, creating an authentic atmosphere where visitors can experience real island culture, participate in traditional activities, and connect with the warm-hearted local people.","Visitors to Dhanbidhoo are drawn by its pristine natural beauty, authentic cultural experiences, traditional fishing heritage, peaceful atmosphere, untouched beaches, and the opportunity to witness genuine Maldivian island life away from commercial tourism. The island offers a unique window into the soul of the Maldives and its enduring traditions."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Dhanbidhoo Island is strategically located in the southern region of Laamu Atoll, approximately 240 kilometers south of Male. This remote location has helped preserve the island''s authentic character and traditional way of life, making it a perfect destination for travelers seeking genuine cultural experiences and pristine natural beauty.","The island''s position within Laamu Atoll provides access to some of the Maldives'' most untouched coral reefs, pristine beaches, and crystal-clear lagoons. The surrounding waters are rich with marine life, making it an excellent base for snorkeling, diving, and traditional fishing experiences with local fishermen."]},{"heading":"Dhanbidhoo: Traditional Fishing Village & Cultural Heritage","paragraphs":["Dhanbidhoo Island embodies the timeless traditions of Maldivian fishing communities, where generations have lived in harmony with the ocean. The island offers visitors authentic insights into traditional island culture, fishing practices, and the close-knit community bonds that define genuine Maldivian life."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing Heritage","paragraphs":["Experience authentic fishing practices with local fishermen using traditional dhonis and time-honored techniques passed down through generations, offering genuine insights into Maldivian maritime culture."]},{"heading":"Authentic Island Culture","paragraphs":["Immerse yourself in genuine Maldivian culture through community interactions, traditional crafts, local customs, and cultural celebrations that showcase the island''s rich heritage and warm hospitality."]},{"heading":"Pristine Natural Beauty","paragraphs":["Discover untouched beaches with powdery white sand, crystal-clear waters, and vibrant coral reefs that remain largely undisturbed by commercial tourism, offering pure tropical paradise."]},{"heading":"Community Experiences","paragraphs":["Participate in daily island life, from traditional cooking and handicrafts to community gatherings and local celebrations that provide authentic connections with island residents."]},{"heading":"Traditional Features","paragraphs":["Authentic elements of Dhanbidhoo''s fishing village culture:"]},{"heading":"Cultural Experiences","paragraphs":["Authentic activities and cultural immersion opportunities:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Dhanbidhoo offers authentic cultural and natural experiences that showcase traditional Maldivian island life. The island''s activities focus on cultural immersion, traditional practices, and pristine natural environments, providing visitors with genuine insights into the heritage and beauty of the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing Adventures","paragraphs":["Join local fishermen on authentic fishing expeditions using traditional dhonis and time-honored techniques, experiencing the maritime heritage that defines Maldivian culture."]},{"heading":"Cultural Immersion Programs","paragraphs":["Participate in authentic cultural exchanges, traditional crafts, local cooking, and community activities that provide deep insights into genuine Maldivian island life."]},{"heading":"Pristine Beach Exploration","paragraphs":["Discover untouched beaches, crystal-clear lagoons, and vibrant coral reefs in their natural state, perfect for swimming, snorkeling, and peaceful relaxation."]},{"heading":"Authentic Island Life","paragraphs":["Experience daily island routines, traditional practices, community gatherings, and local customs that showcase the genuine rhythm of Maldivian island living."]},{"heading":"Marine Life Discovery","paragraphs":["Explore pristine coral reefs, encounter diverse marine life, and discover untouched underwater ecosystems in the crystal-clear waters surrounding the island."]},{"heading":"Traditional Crafts & Skills","paragraphs":["Learn traditional Maldivian crafts, boat building techniques, coconut processing, and other heritage skills from experienced local artisans and craftspeople."]},{"heading":"People & Fishing Community","paragraphs":["Dhanbidhoo is home to approximately 800 residents who represent the authentic heart of traditional Maldivian fishing communities. This close-knit island community has preserved centuries-old traditions while maintaining the warm hospitality and strong community bonds that characterize genuine Maldivian culture and island life."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing Community","paragraphs":["The island''s residents are primarily engaged in traditional fishing, with skills and knowledge passed down through generations. The community maintains traditional dhoni boats, practices sustainable fishing methods, and preserves maritime heritage that has sustained Maldivian communities for centuries, creating an authentic living museum of island culture."]},{"heading":"Cultural Preservation","paragraphs":["Dhanbidhoo''s community is deeply committed to preserving traditional Maldivian culture, language, and customs. The island serves as a guardian of authentic heritage, where traditional music, dance, crafts, and storytelling continue to thrive, offering visitors genuine insights into the cultural soul of the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Community Hospitality","paragraphs":["The legendary Maldivian hospitality is alive and genuine in Dhanbidhoo, where visitors are welcomed as extended family members. The community''s warmth, generosity, and eagerness to share their culture create meaningful connections and authentic experiences that leave lasting impressions on all who visit."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"What makes Dhanbidhoo Island special for cultural experiences?","answer":"Dhanbidhoo Island offers authentic traditional Maldivian fishing village experiences with genuine cultural immersion, traditional fishing practices, pristine natural beauty, and warm community hospitality. The island preserves centuries-old traditions while welcoming visitors to participate in authentic island life, traditional crafts, fishing expeditions, and cultural exchanges that provide deep insights into genuine Maldivian heritage and customs."},{"question":"How do I get to Dhanbidhoo Island in Laamu Atoll?","answer":"Dhanbidhoo can be reached via domestic flight to Kadhdhoo Airport (45 minutes from Male) followed by a 30-minute speedboat transfer, or by direct seaplane (50 minutes from Male). Alternative routes include connections via Gan Island or traditional ferry services from the atoll capital. The journey to this remote location is part of the authentic experience, taking you away from commercial tourism areas."},{"question":"What type of accommodation and experiences are available on Dhanbidhoo?","answer":"Activities include traditional fishing expeditions, cultural programs, handicraft workshops, community interactions, pristine beach exploration, and marine life discovery. The island is perfect for travelers seeking genuine cultural experiences and authentic island life."},{"question":"Is Dhanbidhoo suitable for travelers interested in traditional culture?","answer":"Dhanbidhoo is exceptionally suited for cultural enthusiasts with its authentic fishing village atmosphere, traditional practices, genuine community interactions, and preserved heritage. The island offers traditional dhoni boat experiences, local cooking classes, handicraft workshops, cultural festivals, and opportunities to live alongside local families. Visitors gain authentic insights into Maldivian maritime culture, traditional fishing techniques, and the genuine warmth of island community life."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["fonadhoo","gan","maabaidhoo","mundoo","isdhoo","kunahandhoo","hithadhoo","maamendhoo","maavah"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhanbidhoo';


-- dhangethi
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhangethi Island from Male?","answer":"You can reach Dhangethi Island from Male by public ferry or speedboat. The public ferry operates three times a week (Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday), departing from Male at 9:00 AM and arriving at Dhangethi around 2:30 PM. Some guesthouses also offer private transfers for a higher fee. It''s recommended to book your transfers in advance, especially during high season."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhangethi Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhangethi Island is during the northeast monsoon (December-April), which generally provides the best overall weather conditions with less rainfall, lower humidity, and calmer seas. This period is ideal for snorkeling, diving, and beach activities. The water visibility is excellent during these months, making it perfect for marine life observation. The southwest monsoon (May-November) may have occasional rain showers but still offers many sunny days, fewer tourists, and lower prices. For whale shark sightings, the period from January to April is best. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) year-round, making Dhangethi a viable destination throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Dhangethi different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Dhangethi stands out from other local islands in the Maldives primarily because of its authentic, traditional character that has been well-preserved despite growing tourism. The island is less developed than popular local islands like Maafushi or Thulusdhoo, offering a more genuine cultural experience. Dhangethi has an exceptional house reef accessible directly from shore, with abundant marine life including turtles and reef sharks. The island''s location in Alifu Dhaalu Atoll provides proximity to famous dive sites and whale shark spotting areas. Additionally, Dhangethi is known for its strong fishing heritage and traditional craftsmanship, particularly boat building. The island is less crowded than more established tourist islands, meaning visitors often have beaches and snorkeling spots almost to themselves."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Dhangethi Island?","answer":"Yes, as Dhangethi is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas. Both men and women should cover shoulders and knees when walking around the village, visiting shops, or dining in local restaurants. Swimwear (including bikinis) is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach\" area on the eastern side of the island. When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads with a scarf. These dress code requirements are a sign of respect for local culture and traditions and are generally well-enforced on the island."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Dhangethi?","answer":"Dhangethi is renowned for its exceptional marine biodiversity, and snorkelers can expect to see a wide variety of underwater life directly from the beach. Common sightings include colorful reef fish (parrotfish, angelfish, butterflyfish, triggerfish, etc.), blacktip reef sharks, hawksbill and green sea turtles, eagle rays, stingrays, moray eels, octopuses, and various crustaceans. The house reef features healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. The island''s location in Alifu Dhaalu Atoll also provides opportunities for excursions to nearby areas known for manta ray and whale shark sightings (seasonal). The relatively low number of visitors means marine life is less disturbed and more abundant compared to heavily visited areas, providing exceptional snorkeling experiences directly from the shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhangethi';


-- dharanboodhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dharanboodhoo Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 8-10 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 3-4 hours from Male). Most accommodations can assist with arranging transportation."},{"question":"What is the traditional lacquerware craft of Dharanboodhoo and why is it significant?","answer":"Dharanboodhoo''s traditional lacquerware is an ancient craft that involves creating intricate decorative items using techniques passed down through generations. Artisans work with wood to create base objects, then apply multiple layers of colored lacquer derived from natural materials to create beautiful designs. This craft is significant because it represents one of the oldest continuous artistic traditions in the Maldives, dating back centuries. Historically, Dharanboodhoo lacquerware was used by Maldivian nobility, and specific designs often carry symbolic meanings and cultural references. The island has been known as a center for this craft throughout Maldivian history, with its artisans supplying handicrafts throughout the archipelago. Today, Dharanboodhoo is one of the few places where this traditional craft is still practiced using authentic methods and materials, making it a living repository of this important aspect of Maldivian cultural heritage."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dharanboodhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Dharanboodhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. This period is ideal for water activities, with excellent visibility for snorkeling and diving. The southwest monsoon (May to November) brings occasional rain showers but also lower prices and fewer tourists. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Dharanboodhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Dharanboodhoo stands out for its exceptional preservation of traditional lacquerware craftsmanship. While many Maldivian islands are known for fishing or agriculture, Dharanboodhoo has developed a unique identity centered around its handicraft heritage. This specialized craft has shaped the island''s culture, economy, and community for generations. The island offers visitors a rare opportunity to witness and learn about this traditional art form while also enjoying the pristine beaches and marine environment typical of the Maldives. For travelers interested in cultural heritage alongside natural beauty, Dharanboodhoo provides a distinctive experience that can''t be found on other local islands."},{"question":"What souvenirs should I consider purchasing from Dharanboodhoo?","answer":"Dharanboodhoo offers several unique souvenirs that showcase the island''s cultural heritage and craftsmanship. The most distinctive souvenirs are authentic lacquerware pieces created by local artisans. These include decorative boxes, vases, containers, and ornamental items featuring traditional Maldivian designs with vibrant colors and intricate patterns. When purchasing handicrafts, buy directly from artisans or local craft shops to ensure authenticity and that your purchase supports the local community. Remember that each piece is handmade using traditional techniques, making every item unique. The price reflects the considerable skill, time, and materials involved in creating these pieces. These souvenirs not only provide beautiful mementos of your visit but also help preserve important cultural traditions and support local artisans."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dharanboodhoo';


-- dharavandhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dharavandhoo Island?","answer":"Seaplane transfers are available seasonally, while public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 8 hours). Private speedboat transfers can also be arranged."},{"question":"When is the best time to see whale sharks and manta rays?","answer":"Whale sharks can be seen year-round in Dharavandhoo waters, with peak sightings from August to November. Manta rays are most abundant from May to November during the southwest monsoon, particularly in Hanifaru Bay. The best overall conditions for marine activities are typically from December to April."},{"question":"Do I need to be a certified diver to enjoy the marine life?","answer":"No, many of Dharavandhoo''s marine wonders can be experienced through snorkeling. The house reef offers excellent snorkeling directly from shore, and guided snorkel trips to Hanifaru Bay and whale shark areas are suitable for confident swimmers. For scuba diving, certification is required, but discover scuba programs are available for beginners."},{"question":"What makes Dharavandhoo different from other Maldivian islands?","answer":"Dharavandhoo''s unique combination of factors sets it apart: its location within a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve, reliable whale shark sightings year-round, proximity to Hanifaru Bay''s manta ray gatherings, domestic airport access, and blend of local culture with well-developed tourism infrastructure. Few islands offer this combination of marine life access and convenience."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Dharavandhoo?","answer":"As a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, modest dress is required in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities or at resort day visits."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dharavandhoo';


-- dhevvadhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhevvadhoo Island?","answer":"Due to the island''s remote location in the southern atolls, direct public ferry services from Male are limited. Most guesthouses can arrange the complete transfer from Male, including the domestic flight and speedboat connection. When planning your journey, remember that weather conditions can occasionally affect flight schedules in this region, so it''s advisable to allow buffer days in your travel plans. The journey to Dhevvadhoo is part of the adventure, offering beautiful aerial views of the Maldivian atolls during the flight and a chance to experience the stunning blue waters during the speedboat transfer."},{"question":"What makes Dhevvadhoo''s agricultural traditions unique in the Maldives?","answer":"Dhevvadhoo''s agricultural traditions stand out in the Maldives because most islands in the country focus primarily on fishing due to limited arable land and freshwater resources. Dhevvadhoo has developed agricultural practices specifically adapted to the challenging island environment, including methods to manage the sandy soil, maximize limited freshwater, and protect crops from salt spray. The island grows a diverse range of tropical fruits and vegetables that supplement the traditional seafood-based diet, creating a more varied food system than found on many other islands. These agricultural activities have shaped the island''s landscape, with small farming plots creating pockets of lush vegetation throughout the island. The community has preserved traditional knowledge about planting cycles, seed saving, and natural pest management techniques that have been passed down through generations. This agricultural focus has created a distinctive island identity that balances land and sea resources, offering visitors insights into a less-known aspect of Maldivian culture and sustainable island living."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhevvadhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhevvadhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. This period is ideal for water activities, with excellent visibility for snorkeling and diving. For those interested in experiencing the island''s agricultural traditions, this dry season also coincides with the harvest period for many fruits and vegetables, making it an excellent time to see the farming areas at their most productive. The southwest monsoon (May to November) brings occasional rain showers but also lower prices and fewer tourists. This season can be good for seeing the planting and growing phases of agriculture. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year. Due to Dhevvadhoo''s southern location, it may experience slightly different weather patterns than northern atolls, with the possibility of brief rain showers even during the dry season. The shoulder months of November and April often offer a good balance of favorable weather and lower visitor numbers."},{"question":"What type of accommodation can I expect on Dhevvadhoo?","answer":"Accommodation on Dhevvadhoo is limited and simple, reflecting the island''s authentic character and minimal tourism development. The island offers a small number of family-run guesthouses that provide clean, comfortable rooms with basic amenities including air conditioning, private bathrooms with hot water, and Wi-Fi (though connectivity may be inconsistent). Most accommodations offer full-board packages that include three home-cooked Maldivian meals daily, featuring fresh produce from the island''s farms and seafood caught by local fishermen. Some farming families also offer homestay experiences where guests stay in a room within a family home, providing deeper cultural immersion and the opportunity to participate in agricultural activities. Luxury amenities are limited, and the focus is on authentic experiences rather than high-end facilities. What these accommodations lack in luxury, they make up for in personal service, cultural insights, and genuine hospitality. Visitors should adjust their expectations accordingly and embrace the opportunity to experience authentic island life rather than expecting resort-style amenities."},{"question":"Can I participate in agricultural activities as a visitor to Dhevvadhoo?","answer":"Yes, visitors to Dhevvadhoo have excellent opportunities to participate in traditional agricultural activities, which is one of the island''s unique attractions. Most guesthouses can arrange for guests to join local farmers in their daily activities, from watering and tending to crops to harvesting ripe produce. Farm tours are available where you can learn about the different crops grown on the island and the traditional farming techniques adapted to the island environment. Some accommodations offer more immersive experiences where visitors can participate in the entire process from planting or harvesting to preparing meals with the fresh ingredients. Cooking classes often incorporate freshly harvested produce, allowing you to learn how local ingredients are used in traditional Maldivian cuisine. These activities provide not just entertainment but educational insights into sustainable farming practices and food systems. The level of participation can be adjusted based on your interest and experience, from simple observation to active involvement. Participating in these activities provides a unique experience not commonly available on other Maldivian islands while supporting the local agricultural community."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhevvadhoo';


-- dhidhdhoo-alif-dhaalu
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhidhdhoo Island?","answer":"Dhidhdhoo Island is accessible via several transportation options. The most common route is by public ferry from Male, which operates three times a week (Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday), departing at 9:00 AM and arriving at Dhidhdhoo around 2:30 PM. Alternatively, you can take a speedboat to Mahibadhoo (the atoll capital), followed by a local boat to Dhidhdhoo. For a faster route, you can take a domestic flight from Velana International Airport to Maamigili (about 20 minutes), followed by a speedboat to Dhidhdhoo (approximately 40 minutes). Most guesthouses can assist with arranging transfers and will provide detailed information about the best options based on your arrival date and preferences."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhidhdhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhidhdhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (November to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. January to March typically offers the most reliable weather conditions with minimal rainfall and gentle breezes. The southwest monsoon season (May to October) brings occasional rain showers and slightly rougher seas, though there are still many sunny days. This period offers advantages including fewer tourists, lower prices, and lush island vegetation. If you''re interested in whale shark encounters in the nearby South Ari Marine Protected Area, these gentle giants can be spotted year-round, though sightings may be more consistent from January to April. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year, making Dhidhdhoo a viable destination in any season depending on your preferences."},{"question":"What makes Dhidhdhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Dhidhdhoo distinguishes itself from other local islands through its exceptional preservation of traditional Maldivian life and relatively untouched natural environment. Unlike more developed islands that have seen significant tourism infrastructure, Dhidhdhoo maintains its authentic character with a community still primarily engaged in traditional fishing and island activities. The island''s smaller size and population create an intimate atmosphere where visitors quickly feel welcomed as part of the community rather than just tourists. Dhidhdhoo''s house reef is particularly impressive, with healthy coral formations accessible directly from shore, offering exceptional snorkeling without boat trips. The island''s beaches remain pristine and uncrowded, providing a more secluded experience than busier local islands. Dhidhdhoo also stands out for its strong fishing heritage, with visitors able to observe and participate in traditional fishing practices that have sustained the community for generations. For travelers seeking the increasingly rare experience of authentic Maldivian island life without sacrificing natural beauty or basic comforts, Dhidhdhoo offers a perfect balance that''s becoming harder to find as tourism expands throughout the archipelago."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Dhidhdhoo Island?","answer":"Yes, as Dhidhdhoo is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas. Both men and women should cover shoulders and knees when walking around the village, visiting shops, or dining in local establishments. Men should wear t-shirts/shirts and shorts that reach the knee or longer. Women should wear t-shirts/blouses with sleeves and skirts/shorts/pants that cover the knees. Lightweight, loose-fitting clothing is ideal given the tropical climate. Swimwear (including bikinis) is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach\" area on the northern shore of the island. When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads with a scarf. These dress code requirements are a sign of respect for local culture and traditions. It''s worth noting that on excursions to uninhabited islands, sandbanks, or during resort day trips, Western-style swimwear is generally acceptable. When packing for your trip, include modest clothing for island activities along with your regular beachwear for designated areas."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Dhidhdhoo?","answer":"The waters around Dhidhdhoo offer exceptional marine biodiversity, making it a paradise for snorkelers. The island''s house reef features healthy coral formations with both hard corals (brain coral, staghorn coral, table coral) and colorful soft corals creating a vibrant underwater landscape. Reef fish are abundant, including various species of butterflyfish, angelfish, parrotfish, surgeonfish, and triggerfish displaying spectacular colors. Blacktip and whitetip reef sharks are commonly spotted patrolling the reef edges, particularly in the morning and late afternoon. Green and hawksbill sea turtles can be observed feeding on seagrass beds or resting among coral formations. Various ray species inhabit the surrounding waters, including eagle rays and stingrays often seen gliding over sandy areas adjacent to the reef. Moray eels, octopus, and reef cuttlefish provide fascinating encounters for observant snorkelers exploring coral crevices. During certain seasons, schools of jackfish, tuna, and trevally create impressive underwater spectacles. The nearby channels occasionally attract larger pelagic species, while the South Ari Marine Protected Area offers the possibility of whale shark encounters on dedicated excursions."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhidhdhoo-alif-dhaalu';


-- dhidhdhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhidhdhoo Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 16-18 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 5-6 hours from Hanimaadhoo)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhidhdhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhidhdhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. The northern atolls can experience slightly different weather patterns than the southern regions, but generally follow similar seasonal trends. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Dhidhdhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Dhidhdhoo stands out as the administrative capital of Haa Alifu Atoll, offering a unique blend of government center and traditional island life. Unlike smaller local islands, Dhidhdhoo features better infrastructure including a regional hospital, government offices, and educational facilities. This makes it an excellent base for exploring the northern atolls while enjoying more reliable amenities than typically found on local islands. Despite its administrative role, Dhidhdhoo maintains its island charm and cultural heritage."},{"question":"What facilities and services are available on Dhidhdhoo Island?","answer":"As the atoll capital, Dhidhdhoo offers more facilities than typical local islands. These include a regional hospital providing healthcare services for the entire atoll, government administrative offices, educational facilities, banking services, reliable electricity and internet connectivity, and a wider range of shops and services. Visitors will find more dining options, better communication services, and generally more comfortable infrastructure while still experiencing authentic Maldivian island life."},{"question":"What excursions can I take from Dhidhdhoo Island?","answer":"Dhidhdhoo serves as an excellent base for exploring Haa Alifu Atoll. Popular excursions include visits to historical Utheemu Island (the birthplace of Sultan Mohamed Thakurufaanu, a national hero), trips to uninhabited islands for picnics and snorkeling, diving excursions to pristine reefs, traditional fishing trips, visits to sandbanks, and island hopping to other inhabited islands in the atoll. Most guesthouses can arrange these excursions, often sharing boats with other visitors to make them more affordable."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhidhdhoo';


-- dhiffushi
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Kaafu Atoll, North Male Atoll"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 800 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"0.8 km long, 0.4 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"35 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Public ferry, speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Pristine beaches, excellent snorkeling, authentic culture"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Beach lovers, budget travelers, cultural experiences"}],"island_overview":["Located in the Kaafu Atoll approximately 35 kilometers northeast of Male, Dhiffushi Island offers visitors an authentic Maldivian experience with pristine natural beauty and warm local hospitality. This charming island maintains its traditional character while providing excellent facilities for tourists seeking an affordable yet memorable stay in the Maldives.","What makes Dhiffushi special is its perfect balance of untouched natural beauty and modern amenities. The island features some of the most beautiful beaches in Kaafu Atoll, with powdery white sand and crystal-clear turquoise waters. The surrounding coral reefs are exceptionally healthy, offering world-class snorkeling and diving opportunities right from the shore.","Visitors to Dhiffushi are drawn by its stunning beaches, excellent marine life, authentic cultural experiences, and budget-friendly accommodations. The island''s strategic location provides easy access to other popular destinations in North Male Atoll while offering a peaceful retreat from the busier tourist areas."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Dhiffushi Island is situated in the northeastern part of Kaafu Atoll (North Male Atoll), approximately 35 kilometers from Male. This location provides excellent access to pristine coral reefs and diverse marine ecosystems while maintaining the peaceful atmosphere of a traditional Maldivian island.","The island''s position offers protection from strong currents while still providing access to excellent snorkeling and diving sites. The surrounding lagoon features crystal-clear waters with excellent visibility, making it perfect for underwater exploration and water sports activities."]},{"heading":"Dhiffushi: Pristine Beaches & Natural Paradise","paragraphs":["Dhiffushi Island is renowned for its exceptional natural beauty, featuring some of the most pristine beaches and healthiest coral reefs in Kaafu Atoll. The island offers visitors an unspoiled tropical paradise with crystal-clear waters and abundant marine life."]},{"heading":"Pristine White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Dhiffushi boasts stunning white sand beaches with crystal-clear turquoise waters, offering the perfect setting for relaxation, swimming, and beach activities in a peaceful, uncrowded environment."]},{"heading":"Excellent Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef is easily accessible from shore and features vibrant coral formations with diverse marine life, making it perfect for snorkeling enthusiasts of all skill levels."]},{"heading":"Rich Marine Biodiversity","paragraphs":["The waters around Dhiffushi are home to abundant marine life including colorful reef fish, sea turtles, rays, and occasional reef sharks, providing excellent underwater experiences."]},{"heading":"Authentic Island Life","paragraphs":["Experience genuine Maldivian culture and hospitality in a traditional island setting, with opportunities to interact with friendly locals and learn about island traditions."]},{"heading":"Beach Paradise","paragraphs":["Dhiffushi''s beaches offer exceptional natural beauty:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The island''s marine ecosystem features:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Dhiffushi offers a variety of activities focused on marine adventures, cultural experiences, and natural exploration. The island''s pristine environment and friendly community provide excellent opportunities for both relaxation and adventure."]},{"heading":"Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the vibrant house reef directly from shore or join boat excursions to nearby coral gardens and marine sanctuaries."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving","paragraphs":["Discover underwater wonders at nearby dive sites with professional dive operators offering courses and guided dives."]},{"heading":"Sandbank Visits","paragraphs":["Experience pristine uninhabited sandbanks for picnics, swimming, and the ultimate desert island adventure."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Join local fishermen for traditional night fishing experiences using hand lines under the starlit sky."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping","paragraphs":["Visit neighboring islands in Kaafu Atoll to experience different communities and explore diverse environments."]},{"heading":"Cultural Experiences","paragraphs":["Engage with local community life, learn traditional crafts, and experience authentic Maldivian hospitality."]},{"heading":"People & Community Life","paragraphs":["Dhiffushi is home to a close-knit community of approximately 800 residents who have embraced sustainable tourism while preserving their traditional way of life. The island''s population is known for their exceptional hospitality, strong community bonds, and commitment to maintaining their cultural heritage."]},{"heading":"Daily Life","paragraphs":["Life on Dhiffushi follows traditional Maldivian rhythms, beginning with morning prayers and continuing with fishing, tourism-related work, and community activities. The island maintains a peaceful atmosphere where traditional values coexist harmoniously with modern tourism development. Fishing remains an important livelihood alongside tourism services."]},{"heading":"Culture & Traditions","paragraphs":["Dhiffushi preserves authentic Maldivian cultural traditions including traditional music, dance, and crafts. The community celebrates Islamic festivals with enthusiasm and maintains traditional practices such as boat building, fishing techniques, and local cuisine preparation. Visitors can often observe or participate in cultural activities."]},{"heading":"Community Tourism","paragraphs":["The island has developed a sustainable approach to tourism that benefits the entire community. Many residents are involved in tourism services, from guesthouse operations to tour guiding, creating economic opportunities while maintaining the island''s authentic character and environmental integrity."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhiffushi Island?","answer":"Dhiffushi Island can be reached by public ferry or speedboat from Male. Most guesthouses can arrange speedboat transfers for guests."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhiffushi?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhiffushi is during the dry season from December to April when you''ll experience sunny skies, calm seas, and minimal rainfall. This period offers the best conditions for beach activities, snorkeling, and diving. However, Dhiffushi can be visited year-round, with the wet season (May to November) offering lower prices and fewer crowds, though with occasional rain showers and rougher seas."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Dhiffushi?","answer":"Dhiffushi offers various budget-friendly and mid-range accommodations including family-run guesthouses, boutique hotels, and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, meal packages, and can arrange tours and transfers. Many properties have designated areas where Western-style swimwear is permitted."},{"question":"What activities can I do on Dhiffushi Island?","answer":"Dhiffushi offers excellent snorkeling directly from the beach with vibrant house reefs and diverse marine life. Other popular activities include scuba diving at nearby sites, sandbank excursions, traditional night fishing, island hopping tours, cultural experiences with the local community, and simply relaxing on pristine beaches. The island is perfect for those seeking authentic Maldivian experiences combined with natural beauty."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["himmafushi","huraa","maafushi","gulhi","guraidhoo","hulhumale","male","kaashidhoo","gaafaru"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhiffushi';


-- dhiggaru
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Meemu Atoll, Central Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 900 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.1 km long, 0.5 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"135 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Domestic flight + speedboat (1.5 hours total)"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Pristine beaches, fishing culture, authentic experiences"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Culture seekers, nature lovers, budget travelers"}],"island_overview":["Nestled in the pristine waters of Meemu Atoll, Dhiggaru Island offers visitors an authentic Maldivian experience away from the crowds. This charming island combines natural beauty with traditional island life.","Dhiggaru is known for its long stretches of white sand beaches, excellent fishing culture, and peaceful atmosphere. The island maintains its traditional character while welcoming visitors with genuine hospitality.","What makes Dhiggaru special is its unspoiled environment, rich marine biodiversity, and the opportunity to experience local culture in a relaxed setting."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Dhiggaru Island is located in the central part of Meemu Atoll, approximately 135 kilometers from Male. This location offers a peaceful retreat while maintaining access to excellent marine environments.","The island''s position provides natural protection from strong currents while offering access to vibrant coral reefs and abundant marine life."]},{"heading":"Dhiggaru: Natural Beauty & Marine Life","paragraphs":["Dhiggaru Island boasts stunning natural beauty with powder-soft white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and healthy coral reefs teeming with marine life."]},{"heading":"Pristine White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Dhiggaru features some of the most beautiful beaches in Meemu Atoll, perfect for relaxation and beach walks."]},{"heading":"Excellent Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef offers easy access to colorful coral formations and diverse marine life."]},{"heading":"Authentic Fishing Culture","paragraphs":["Experience the traditional fishing lifestyle that has sustained Dhiggaru for generations."]},{"heading":"Sandbank Visits","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks for the ultimate tropical island experience."]},{"heading":"Beach Highlights","paragraphs":["Dhiggaru''s coastal features include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The island''s underwater world features:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Dhiggaru offers a variety of activities focused on marine adventures, cultural experiences, and relaxation."]},{"heading":"Snorkeling Adventures","paragraphs":["Explore vibrant coral reefs teeming with tropical fish."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving","paragraphs":["Discover world-class dive sites in Meemu Atoll."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands to experience different aspects of the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Fishing Excursions","paragraphs":["Join local fishermen for traditional fishing experiences."]},{"heading":"Cultural Experiences","paragraphs":["Learn about traditional Maldivian crafts and daily life."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy breathtaking sunsets over the Indian Ocean."]},{"heading":"People & Community Life","paragraphs":["Dhiggaru is home to a close-knit community of approximately 900 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing sustainable tourism."]},{"heading":"Daily Life","paragraphs":["Life on Dhiggaru revolves around fishing, tourism, and community activities. The island has a peaceful rhythm that reflects traditional Maldivian values."]},{"heading":"Culture & Traditions","paragraphs":["Dhiggaru preserves authentic Maldivian cultural traditions including music, dance, and handicrafts. The community celebrates Islamic festivals with enthusiasm."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhiggaru Island?","answer":"Dhiggaru can be reached by domestic flight to Kadhdhoo Airport (1 hour from Male) followed by a 30-minute speedboat ride. Seaplane transfers are also available but depend on weather conditions."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhiggaru?","answer":"The best time is during the dry season from December to April, but Dhiggaru can be visited year-round."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["kolhufushi","muli","maduvvaree","raimmandhoo","naalaafushi","veyvah"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhiggaru';


-- dhigurah
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"What''s the best time of year to see whale sharks near Dhigurah?","answer":"Unlike many other locations where whale shark sightings are seasonal, Dhigurah offers year-round opportunities to see these magnificent creatures. The South Ari Atoll Marine Protected Area hosts a resident population of whale sharks, meaning you have a good chance of encounters regardless of when you visit. That said, sea conditions are generally calmer from December to April during the northeast monsoon, making this period ideal for water activities. During the southwest monsoon (May-November), sightings remain frequent though sea conditions can occasionally be rougher."},{"question":"How do I get to Dhigurah Island from Male?","answer":"There are three main ways to reach Dhigurah from Male:\n    \n      Public Ferry: The most economical option, operating twice weekly (Monday and Thursday) from Male''s Villingili Ferry Terminal. Speedboat: Faster but more expensive, taking about 2.5-3 hours. Most guesthouses can help arrange transfers when you book accommodation."},{"question":"Is Dhigurah suitable for families with children?","answer":"Dhigurah can be a great destination for families, with some considerations:\n    \n      Pros: Safe environment, gentle lagoon for swimming, fascinating marine life, cultural learning opportunities, and more affordable than resorts. Considerations: Limited medical facilities, modest accommodation standards, limited dining options, and the need to respect local customs. Many guesthouses welcome families, though facilities like pools and kids'' clubs are uncommon. Whale shark snorkeling is generally suitable for confident child swimmers (with life jackets available). The island''s relaxed pace and natural wonders can make for a memorable family adventure."},{"question":"What should I pack for a trip to Dhigurah?","answer":"Essential items to pack for Dhigurah include:\n    \n      Swimwear & beach cover-ups (remember modest dress required outside bikini beach)\n      Reef-safe sunscreen to protect marine life\n      Water shoes for walking on coral and hot sand\n      Snorkeling gear (though most guesthouses provide this)\n      Light, modest clothing for village areas\n      Cash (US dollars or Maldivian rufiyaa) as there''s no ATM\n      Basic medications as pharmacy options are limited\n      Waterproof camera or phone case for marine life encounters\n      Power adapter (UK-style plugs are standard)\n    \n    Most guesthouses provide basic toiletries, towels, and drinking water."},{"question":"How does Dhigurah compare to resort islands?","answer":"Dhigurah offers a very different experience from resort islands:\n    \n      \n        \n          Feature\n          Dhigurah\n          Resorts\n        \n      \n      \n        \n          Cost\n          Budget-friendly\n          Premium prices\n        \n        \n          Atmosphere\n          Authentic local culture\n          Luxury vacation\n        \n        \n          Alcohol\n          Not available\n          Available\n        \n        \n          Marine Life\n          Abundant, natural encounters\n          Varies by resort\n        \n        \n          Dress Code\n          Modest outside bikini beach\n          No restrictions\n        \n        \n          Activities\n          Local experiences\n          Resort amenities\n        \n      \n    \n    Many visitors combine both experiences - staying at a local island like Dhigurah for part of their trip and a resort for the remainder."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhigurah';


-- dhiyamigili
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Thaa Atoll, Central Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 1,200 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.4 km long, 0.9 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"201 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Seaplane or domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Serene culture, tranquil beaches, excellent snorkeling"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Culture enthusiasts, beach lovers, snorkelers, peace seekers"}],"island_overview":["Dhiyamigili Island is a serene local island in Thaa Atoll that offers visitors a peaceful glimpse into traditional Maldivian life. This tranquil island combines natural beauty with a harmonious atmosphere, providing an ideal destination for travelers seeking restful cultural experiences and calming tropical surroundings.","Known for its beautiful beaches, serene coral reefs, and excellent marine biodiversity, Dhiyamigili provides wonderful opportunities for snorkeling, swimming, and underwater exploration. The island''s peaceful community embraces visitors with traditional Maldivian warmth and shares their tranquil way of life with serene hospitality.","What makes Dhiyamigili special is its serene natural environment and peaceful island setting. Visitors can enjoy tranquil beaches, explore rich marine ecosystems, and experience harmonious island culture while staying in comfortable accommodations that showcase the serene Maldivian lifestyle."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Dhiyamigili Island is beautifully located in the central part of Thaa Atoll, approximately 201 kilometers from Male. This location provides a serene and peaceful experience while maintaining good accessibility to the capital and international airport through various transfer options.","The island''s position offers excellent protection from strong currents while providing access to tranquil coral reefs and diverse marine ecosystems. Its location in the atoll makes it an ideal base for exploring the natural beauty and cultural richness of Thaa Atoll in its most serene form."]},{"heading":"Dhiyamigili: Serene Paradise & Marine Tranquility","paragraphs":["Dhiyamigili Island is blessed with exceptional natural beauty, featuring tranquil white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and one of the most serene marine environments in Thaa Atoll. The island offers perfect conditions for beach relaxation and marine exploration in a peaceful, harmonious setting."]},{"heading":"Tranquil White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Dhiyamigili features serene white sand beaches with gentle slopes and calm waters, perfect for swimming, sunbathing, and peaceful beach activities."]},{"heading":"Serene House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef is easily accessible from the beach and offers peaceful snorkeling with diverse marine life and colorful coral formations."]},{"heading":"Peaceful Island Community","paragraphs":["Experience the harmonious spirit of traditional island life with friendly locals and serene Maldivian culture in a peaceful community setting."]},{"heading":"Tranquil Sandbank Adventures","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks and serene islands around Dhiyamigili for peaceful tropical paradise experiences with rich marine life."]},{"heading":"Beach Features","paragraphs":["Dhiyamigili''s coastal highlights include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Dhiyamigili feature:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Dhiyamigili offers a perfect blend of marine adventures, cultural experiences, and serene activities. The island''s peaceful environment and harmonious atmosphere provide ideal conditions for various activities in a tranquil, restful setting."]},{"heading":"Serene House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the peaceful house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility in serene waters."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Excursions","paragraphs":["Discover nearby tranquil dive sites with professional dive operators offering guided dives and certification courses in serene marine environments."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Adventures","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands, tranquil sandbanks, and discover the natural beauty of Thaa Atoll with peaceful exploration opportunities."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience authentic Maldivian fishing techniques with local fishermen during sunset and night fishing trips in serene community settings."]},{"heading":"Peaceful Cultural Immersion","paragraphs":["Learn about local traditions, participate in community activities, and experience serene Maldivian island life with harmonious cultural exchanges."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy tranquil sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing in serene waters."]},{"heading":"Community Life & Culture","paragraphs":["Dhiyamigili is home to approximately 1,200 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing sustainable tourism. The island''s community is known for their exceptional friendliness, hospitality, and serene cultural traditions that create a peaceful atmosphere for visitors."]},{"heading":"Island Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Dhiyamigili follows traditional patterns centered around fishing, family, and community activities. The island maintains a serene atmosphere where visitors can experience peaceful Maldivian culture and customs in their most tranquil, harmonious setting."]},{"heading":"Local Traditions","paragraphs":["The community preserves traditional Maldivian practices including traditional music, dance, handicrafts, and fishing techniques. Visitors can observe and often participate in cultural activities and celebrations in this serene island environment."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Tourism","paragraphs":["Dhiyamigili has embraced sustainable tourism practices that benefit the local community while preserving the island''s natural environment and serene cultural heritage for future generations to experience and enjoy."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhiyamigili Island?","answer":"Regular ferry services are also available from Male and nearby islands in Thaa Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhiyamigili?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhiyamigili is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable for beach activities and water sports. However, the island can be visited year-round with the wet season offering fewer crowds and a more serene local atmosphere."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Dhiyamigili?","answer":"Dhiyamigili offers various accommodations including serene guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages in a peaceful setting."},{"question":"What makes Dhiyamigili''s atmosphere special?","answer":"Dhiyamigili is known for its serene and peaceful natural environment. The island maintains traditional Maldivian cultural practices while offering a harmonious atmosphere perfect for restful exploration. Visitors can experience tranquil cultural exchanges and participate in serene community activities."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["guraidhoo","gaadhiffushi","burunee","vilufushi","veymandoo","vandhoo","thimarafushi","omadhoo","madifushi","kinbidhoo","kandoodhoo","hirilandhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhiyamigili';


-- dhonfanu
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhonfanu Island?","answer":"Dhonfanu Island is accessible via several transportation options. The most common route is by public ferry from Male, which operates twice a week (Monday and Thursday), departing at 8:30 AM and arriving at Dhonfanu around 3:30 PM. For a faster route, you can take a domestic flight from Velana International Airport to Dharavandhoo (about 30 minutes), followed by a speedboat to Dhonfanu (approximately 25 minutes). Most guesthouses can assist with arranging transfers and will provide detailed information about the best options based on your arrival date and preferences."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhonfanu Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhonfanu Island depends on your interests. For general beach and snorkeling activities, the northeast monsoon season (November to April) brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. January to March typically offers the most reliable weather conditions with minimal rainfall. If you''re specifically interested in manta ray and whale shark encounters at nearby Hanifaru Bay, plan your visit between May and November, with peak season being June to October when plankton concentrations attract these magnificent creatures. The southwest monsoon season (May to October) brings occasional rain showers and slightly rougher seas, though there are still many sunny days. This period offers advantages including fewer tourists, lower prices, and the chance to witness the spectacular marine life aggregations in the UNESCO Biosphere Reserve. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year, making Dhonfanu a viable destination in any season depending on your preferences."},{"question":"What makes Dhonfanu different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Dhonfanu distinguishes itself from other local islands through its strategic location within the UNESCO Biosphere Reserve and its exceptional preservation of traditional Maldivian life. Unlike more developed islands that have seen significant tourism infrastructure, Dhonfanu maintains its authentic character with a community still primarily engaged in traditional fishing and island activities. The island''s proximity to Hanifaru Bay, one of the world''s most significant marine protected areas for manta rays and whale sharks, provides visitors with unique opportunities to witness these magnificent creatures during the season. Dhonfanu''s house reef is particularly impressive, with healthy coral formations accessible directly from shore, offering exceptional snorkeling without boat trips. The island''s beaches remain pristine and uncrowded, providing a more secluded experience than busier local islands. Dhonfanu also stands out for its strong fishing heritage, with visitors able to observe and participate in traditional fishing practices that have sustained the community for generations. For travelers seeking the increasingly rare experience of authentic Maldivian island life without sacrificing natural beauty or basic comforts, Dhonfanu offers a perfect balance that''s becoming harder to find as tourism expands throughout the archipelago."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Dhonfanu Island?","answer":"Yes, as Dhonfanu is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas. Both men and women should cover shoulders and knees when walking around the village, visiting shops, or dining in local establishments. Men should wear t-shirts/shirts and shorts that reach the knee or longer. Women should wear t-shirts/blouses with sleeves and skirts/shorts/pants that cover the knees. Lightweight, loose-fitting clothing is ideal given the tropical climate. Swimwear (including bikinis) is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach\" area on the northern shore of the island. When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads with a scarf. These dress code requirements are a sign of respect for local culture and traditions. It''s worth noting that on excursions to uninhabited islands, sandbanks, or during resort day trips, Western-style swimwear is generally acceptable. When packing for your trip, include modest clothing for island activities along with your regular beachwear for designated areas."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Dhonfanu?","answer":"The waters around Dhonfanu offer exceptional marine biodiversity, making it a paradise for snorkelers. The island''s house reef features healthy coral formations with both hard corals (brain coral, staghorn coral, table coral) and colorful soft corals creating a vibrant underwater landscape. Reef fish are abundant, including various species of butterflyfish, angelfish, parrotfish, surgeonfish, and triggerfish displaying spectacular colors. Blacktip and whitetip reef sharks are commonly spotted patrolling the reef edges, particularly in the morning and late afternoon. Green and hawksbill sea turtles can be observed feeding on seagrass beds or resting among coral formations. Various ray species inhabit the surrounding waters, including eagle rays and stingrays often seen gliding over sandy areas adjacent to the reef. Moray eels, octopus, and reef cuttlefish provide fascinating encounters for observant snorkelers exploring coral crevices. During certain seasons, schools of jackfish, tuna, and trevally create impressive underwater spectacles. The nearby Hanifaru Bay (accessible by boat excursion) offers the opportunity to witness manta ray and whale shark aggregations during the southwest monsoon season (May-November)."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhonfanu';


-- dhuvaafaru
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Raa Atoll, Northern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 2,500 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.8 km long, 1.0 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"165 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Seaplane or domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Modern infrastructure, beautiful beaches, progressive community"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Modern travelers, beach lovers, cultural enthusiasts, comfort seekers"}],"island_overview":["Dhuvaafaru Island is a modern local island in Raa Atoll that offers visitors a unique glimpse into contemporary Maldivian life. This progressive island combines natural beauty with modern amenities, providing an ideal destination for travelers seeking comfortable cultural experiences and beautiful tropical surroundings.","Known for its well-planned infrastructure, beautiful beaches, and excellent marine environment, Dhuvaafaru provides wonderful opportunities for snorkeling, swimming, and marine exploration. The island''s welcoming community embraces visitors with modern Maldivian hospitality while maintaining their cultural heritage.","What makes Dhuvaafaru special is its modern development and contemporary island setting. Visitors can enjoy beautiful beaches, explore diverse marine life, and experience progressive island culture while staying in comfortable accommodations that showcase the modern Maldivian lifestyle."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Dhuvaafaru Island is strategically located in the northern part of Raa Atoll, approximately 165 kilometers from Male. This location provides a modern and comfortable experience while maintaining good accessibility to the capital and international airport through various transfer options.","The island''s position offers excellent protection from strong currents while providing access to beautiful coral reefs and diverse marine ecosystems. Its location in the atoll makes it an ideal base for exploring the natural beauty and cultural richness of Raa Atoll."]},{"heading":"Dhuvaafaru: Modern Paradise & Marine Beauty","paragraphs":["Dhuvaafaru Island is blessed with exceptional natural beauty, featuring beautiful white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and one of the most well-maintained marine environments in Raa Atoll. The island offers perfect conditions for beach relaxation and marine exploration in a modern, comfortable setting."]},{"heading":"Beautiful White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Dhuvaafaru features well-maintained white sand beaches with gentle slopes and calm waters, perfect for swimming, sunbathing, and beach activities."]},{"heading":"Excellent House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef is easily accessible from the beach and offers excellent snorkeling with diverse marine life and colorful coral formations."]},{"heading":"Modern Island Community","paragraphs":["Experience the progressive spirit of modern island life with friendly locals and contemporary Maldivian culture in a well-developed community setting."]},{"heading":"Beautiful Sandbank Adventures","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks and beautiful islands around Dhuvaafaru for wonderful tropical paradise experiences with rich marine life."]},{"heading":"Beach Features","paragraphs":["Dhuvaafaru''s coastal highlights include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Dhuvaafaru feature:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Dhuvaafaru offers a perfect blend of marine adventures, cultural experiences, and modern activities. The island''s contemporary environment and progressive atmosphere provide ideal conditions for various activities in a comfortable, well-developed setting."]},{"heading":"Excellent House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the beautiful house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility in clear waters."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Excursions","paragraphs":["Discover nearby beautiful dive sites with professional dive operators offering guided dives and certification courses in well-maintained marine environments."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Adventures","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands, beautiful sandbanks, and discover the natural beauty of Raa Atoll with comfortable exploration opportunities."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience authentic Maldivian fishing techniques with local fishermen during sunset and night fishing trips in modern community settings."]},{"heading":"Modern Cultural Immersion","paragraphs":["Learn about local traditions, participate in community activities, and experience contemporary Maldivian island life with modern cultural exchanges."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy magical sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing in beautiful waters."]},{"heading":"Community Life & Culture","paragraphs":["Dhuvaafaru is home to approximately 2,500 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing modern development and sustainable tourism. The island''s community is known for their exceptional friendliness, hospitality, and progressive cultural approach that creates a comfortable atmosphere for visitors."]},{"heading":"Island Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Dhuvaafaru follows modern patterns while maintaining traditional values centered around fishing, family, and community activities. The island maintains a progressive atmosphere where visitors can experience contemporary Maldivian culture and customs in a well-developed, modern setting."]},{"heading":"Local Traditions","paragraphs":["The community preserves traditional Maldivian practices while embracing modern conveniences including traditional music, dance, handicrafts, and fishing techniques. Visitors can observe and often participate in cultural activities and celebrations in this modern island environment."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Development","paragraphs":["Dhuvaafaru has embraced sustainable development practices that benefit the local community while preserving the island''s natural environment and cultural heritage for future generations to experience and enjoy in a modern context."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Dhuvaafaru Island?","answer":"Regular ferry services are also available from Male and nearby islands in Raa Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Dhuvaafaru?","answer":"The best time to visit Dhuvaafaru is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable for beach activities and water sports. However, the island can be visited year-round with the wet season offering fewer crowds and a more relaxed local atmosphere."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Dhuvaafaru?","answer":"Dhuvaafaru offers various modern accommodations including contemporary guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages in a modern setting."},{"question":"What makes Dhuvaafaru''s community special?","answer":"Dhuvaafaru is known for its modern and progressive community atmosphere. The island maintains traditional Maldivian cultural practices while embracing contemporary development. Visitors can experience modern cultural exchanges and participate in community activities in this well-developed setting."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["rasgetheemu","ungoofaaru","rasmaadhoo","vaadhoo","alifushi","angolhitheemu","fainu","hulhudhuffaaru","inguraidhoo","innamaadhoo","kinolhas","maakurathu","maduvvaree","meedhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'dhuvaafaru';


-- eydhafushi
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Eydhafushi Island?","answer":"Eydhafushi Island is accessible via several transportation options. The most common route is by public ferry from Male, which operates three times a week (Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday), departing at 8:30 AM and arriving at Eydhafushi around 3:00 PM. The most convenient but expensive option is to take a domestic flight from Velana International Airport to Dharavandhoo Airport (approximately 30 minutes), followed by a 15-minute speedboat to Eydhafushi. Most guesthouses and hotels on Eydhafushi can arrange transfers and will provide detailed information about the best options based on your arrival date and preferences. As the atoll capital, Eydhafushi has better transportation connections than most local islands in Baa Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Eydhafushi Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Eydhafushi Island depends on your interests. For general sightseeing and comfortable weather, the northeast monsoon season (November to April) brings dry, sunny conditions with lower humidity and calmer seas. This period is ideal for island exploration and water activities, with January to March typically offering the most reliable weather conditions. If you''re specifically interested in manta ray and whale shark encounters at nearby Hanifaru Bay, plan your visit between May and November, with peak season being June to October when plankton concentrations attract these magnificent creatures. This coincides with the southwest monsoon season, which brings occasional rain showers and slightly rougher seas, though there are still many sunny days. As the atoll capital, Eydhafushi has activities and amenities available year-round, making it less seasonal than some destinations. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year. For the best balance of good weather and marine life encounters, consider visiting during the transitional months of April-May or October-November when you might experience the benefits of both seasons."},{"question":"What makes Eydhafushi different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Eydhafushi distinguishes itself from other local islands through its status as the administrative capital of Baa Atoll. This role gives the island several unique characteristics: First, it has more developed infrastructure and amenities than typical local islands, including government offices, a hospital, educational institutions, banking services, and a wider range of shops and restaurants. Second, Eydhafushi serves as a transportation hub with better connections to Male and surrounding islands, making it more accessible and convenient as a base for exploration. Third, the island plays a central role in managing the UNESCO Biosphere Reserve, hosting conservation offices and environmental initiatives that create a strong awareness of sustainability. Fourth, while smaller islands might offer more pristine beaches, Eydhafushi compensates with its strategic location providing easy access to Hanifaru Bay and other natural wonders of Baa Atoll. Fifth, the island has a larger, more diverse population creating a dynamic community with a blend of traditional and contemporary Maldivian life. For travelers, this means enjoying more comfortable amenities and services while still experiencing authentic culture, plus the convenience of organized excursions to natural attractions throughout the atoll. Eydhafushi is particularly suitable for visitors who want a comfortable base with good facilities while exploring the UNESCO Biosphere Reserve."},{"question":"What activities can I do from Eydhafushi Island?","answer":"Eydhafushi offers an excellent base for a wide range of activities throughout Baa Atoll. The most popular excursion is to Hanifaru Bay (seasonal, May-November), where visitors can snorkel with manta rays and whale sharks during plankton blooms. Snorkeling trips to nearby reefs and marine hotspots are available year-round, with local guides knowing the best locations based on current conditions. Island hopping excursions allow you to visit other inhabited islands like Dhonfanu, Thulhaadhoo (known for lacquerware crafts), and Fulhadhoo with its pristine beaches. Uninhabited island and sandbank picnics offer the quintessential desert island experience with pristine beaches and crystal-clear waters. Traditional fishing trips, both day and night versions, provide cultural insights while catching dinner. Dolphin watching cruises, especially at sunset, offer opportunities to see spinner dolphins and other species performing acrobatic displays. Cultural experiences on Eydhafushi itself include handicraft demonstrations, cooking classes, and community events. Day visits to nearby luxury resorts like Soneva Fushi or Milaidhoo can be arranged for those wanting to experience resort amenities. Water sports including kayaking, stand-up paddleboarding, and windsurfing are available through some guesthouses and tour operators. As the atoll capital, Eydhafushi has several tour operators who can arrange custom excursions based on your interests."},{"question":"What marine life can I see in Baa Atoll?","answer":"Baa Atoll''s designation as a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve reflects its exceptional marine biodiversity. The most famous seasonal phenomenon occurs at Hanifaru Bay, where plankton blooms (May-November) attract feeding aggregations of manta rays and whale sharks. Sometimes dozens of mantas can be seen in this marine protected area, creating an unforgettable snorkeling experience. Throughout the atoll, coral reefs feature both hard corals (brain coral, staghorn coral, table coral) and colorful soft corals creating vibrant underwater landscapes. Reef fish are abundant, including various species of butterflyfish, angelfish, parrotfish, surgeonfish, and triggerfish displaying spectacular colors. Several shark species inhabit the atoll''s waters, with blacktip and whitetip reef sharks commonly spotted around reef edges and channels. Green and hawksbill sea turtles are frequently encountered feeding on seagrass beds or resting among coral formations. Various ray species including eagle rays, stingrays, and occasionally mobula rays glide over sandy areas and reef flats. Moray eels, octopus, and reef cuttlefish provide fascinating encounters for observant snorkelers exploring coral crevices. Pelagic species like tuna, jackfish, and barracuda form impressive schools in deeper waters and channels. Dolphin species, particularly spinner dolphins, are commonly seen in the channels between islands."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'eydhafushi';


-- fainu
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Raa Atoll, Northern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 1,100 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.0 km long, 0.6 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"143 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Seaplane or domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Exquisite culture, gorgeous beaches, excellent snorkeling"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Culture enthusiasts, beach lovers, snorkelers, elegance seekers"}],"island_overview":["Fainu Island is an exquisite local island in Raa Atoll that offers visitors a refined glimpse into traditional Maldivian life. This elegant island combines natural beauty with a sophisticated atmosphere, providing an ideal destination for travelers seeking graceful cultural experiences and gorgeous tropical surroundings.","Known for its beautiful beaches, exquisite coral reefs, and excellent marine biodiversity, Fainu provides wonderful opportunities for snorkeling, swimming, and underwater exploration. The island''s refined community embraces visitors with traditional Maldivian warmth and shares their elegant way of life with exquisite hospitality.","What makes Fainu special is its exquisite natural environment and refined island setting. Visitors can enjoy gorgeous beaches, explore rich marine ecosystems, and experience elegant island culture while staying in comfortable accommodations that showcase the exquisite Maldivian lifestyle."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Fainu Island is elegantly located in the northern part of Raa Atoll, approximately 143 kilometers from Male. This location provides an exquisite and refined experience while maintaining good accessibility to the capital and international airport through various transfer options.","The island''s position offers excellent protection from strong currents while providing access to gorgeous coral reefs and diverse marine ecosystems. Its location in the atoll makes it an ideal base for exploring the natural beauty and cultural richness of Raa Atoll in its most exquisite form."]},{"heading":"Fainu: Exquisite Paradise & Marine Elegance","paragraphs":["Fainu Island is blessed with exceptional natural beauty, featuring gorgeous white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and one of the most exquisite marine environments in Raa Atoll. The island offers perfect conditions for beach relaxation and marine exploration in a refined, elegant setting."]},{"heading":"Gorgeous White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Fainu features exquisite white sand beaches with gentle slopes and calm waters, perfect for swimming, sunbathing, and elegant beach activities."]},{"heading":"Exquisite House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef is easily accessible from the beach and offers refined snorkeling with diverse marine life and colorful coral formations."]},{"heading":"Refined Island Community","paragraphs":["Experience the elegant spirit of traditional island life with friendly locals and exquisite Maldivian culture in a refined community setting."]},{"heading":"Elegant Sandbank Adventures","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks and gorgeous islands around Fainu for exquisite tropical paradise experiences with rich marine life."]},{"heading":"Beach Features","paragraphs":["Fainu''s coastal highlights include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Fainu feature:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Fainu offers a perfect blend of marine adventures, cultural experiences, and exquisite activities. The island''s refined environment and elegant atmosphere provide ideal conditions for various activities in a gorgeous, sophisticated setting."]},{"heading":"Exquisite House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the refined house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility in exquisite waters."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Excursions","paragraphs":["Discover nearby gorgeous dive sites with professional dive operators offering guided dives and certification courses in exquisite marine environments."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Adventures","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands, gorgeous sandbanks, and discover the natural beauty of Raa Atoll with refined exploration opportunities."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience authentic Maldivian fishing techniques with local fishermen during sunset and night fishing trips in exquisite community settings."]},{"heading":"Refined Cultural Immersion","paragraphs":["Learn about local traditions, participate in community activities, and experience exquisite Maldivian island life with elegant cultural exchanges."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy gorgeous sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing in exquisite waters."]},{"heading":"Community Life & Culture","paragraphs":["Fainu is home to approximately 1,100 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing sustainable tourism. The island''s community is known for their exceptional friendliness, hospitality, and exquisite cultural traditions that create a refined atmosphere for visitors."]},{"heading":"Island Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Fainu follows traditional patterns centered around fishing, family, and community activities. The island maintains an exquisite atmosphere where visitors can experience refined Maldivian culture and customs in their most elegant, sophisticated setting."]},{"heading":"Local Traditions","paragraphs":["The community preserves traditional Maldivian practices including traditional music, dance, handicrafts, and fishing techniques. Visitors can observe and often participate in cultural activities and celebrations in this exquisite island environment."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Tourism","paragraphs":["Fainu has embraced sustainable tourism practices that benefit the local community while preserving the island''s natural environment and exquisite cultural heritage for future generations to experience and enjoy."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Fainu Island?","answer":"Regular ferry services are also available from Male and nearby islands in Raa Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Fainu?","answer":"The best time to visit Fainu is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable for beach activities and water sports. However, the island can be visited year-round with the wet season offering fewer crowds and a more exquisite local atmosphere."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Fainu?","answer":"Fainu offers various accommodations including exquisite guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages in a refined setting."},{"question":"What makes Fainu''s elegance special?","answer":"Fainu is known for its exquisite and refined natural environment. The island maintains traditional Maldivian cultural practices while offering an elegant atmosphere perfect for sophisticated exploration. Visitors can experience graceful cultural exchanges and participate in exquisite community activities."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["rasgetheemu","ungoofaaru","rasmaadhoo","vaadhoo","alifushi","angolhitheemu","dhuvaafaru","hulhudhuffaaru","inguraidhoo","innamaadhoo","kinolhas","maakurathu","maduvvaree","meedhoo"]}'::jsonb
