-- fares-maathodaa
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Faresmaathodaa Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 18-20 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 7.5 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Faresmaathodaa Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Faresmaathodaa Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. For whale shark encounters, visit during the southwest monsoon (May to December). Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Faresmaathodaa different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Faresmaathodaa stands out for its exceptional preservation of traditional Maldivian life in a remote setting and its unique twin-island structure. Unlike most Maldivian islands, Faresmaathodaa consists of two separate islands connected by a small bridge, creating a distinctive figure-eight shape. This unique geography provides additional shoreline and beach access, while the channel between the islands creates a natural harbor and a special marine environment."},{"question":"Can I walk between the two parts of Faresmaathodaa Island?","answer":"Yes, the two parts of Faresmaathodaa Island are connected by a small bridge that allows easy pedestrian access between them. Walking across this bridge is actually a popular activity for visitors, as it offers excellent views of the channel and surrounding waters. The bridge has become a gathering spot for locals and visitors alike, especially during sunset when the views are particularly spectacular."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Faresmaathodaa?","answer":"Faresmaathodaa''s waters offer excellent marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip and whitetip), sea turtles, various ray species, and abundant reef fish. The channel between the two parts of the island creates a unique marine environment with different currents and marine life. Seasonal visitors include whale sharks (May-December) and manta rays. The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'fares-maathodaa';


-- feeali
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Feeali Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 12 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 5 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Feeali Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Feeali Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Feeali different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Feeali stands out for its strong fishing tradition and authentic island life. Unlike more tourist-developed islands, Feeali maintains its traditional character with a community deeply connected to fishing. The island offers excellent fishing experiences and genuine cultural exchange away from mass tourism."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Feeali Island?","answer":"As Feeali is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Feeali?","answer":"Feeali''s waters offer good marine biodiversity, including coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip), various reef fish, and occasional sea turtles. The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'feeali';


-- feevah
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Shaviyani Atoll, Northern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 600 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"0.7 km long, 0.3 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"280 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Pristine beaches, excellent fishing, peaceful atmosphere"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Nature lovers, cultural travelers, budget travelers"}],"island_overview":["Nestled in the pristine waters of Shaviyani Atoll, Feevah Island represents the essence of authentic Maldivian island life. This charming local island offers visitors a unique opportunity to experience traditional culture while enjoying some of the most beautiful beaches in the northern Maldives.","Feevah stands out for its untouched natural beauty, crystal-clear lagoons, and vibrant marine life. The island maintains its traditional character while welcoming visitors with genuine Maldivian hospitality and warmth.","What makes Feevah truly special is its peaceful atmosphere, excellent marine life, and the opportunity to experience authentic island culture. Visitors can enjoy budget-friendly accommodations, delicious local cuisine, and unforgettable underwater adventures."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Feevah Island is located in the northern part of Shaviyani Atoll, approximately 280 kilometers from Male. This remote location ensures an authentic and peaceful experience, far from the bustling tourist areas, while still offering excellent accessibility.","The island''s position provides natural protection from strong currents while maintaining access to some of the best fishing spots in the Maldives. The surrounding waters feature healthy coral reefs and abundant marine life."]},{"heading":"Feevah: Natural Beauty & Marine Wonders","paragraphs":["Feevah Island is renowned for its exceptional natural beauty, featuring powder-white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and vibrant marine life. The island offers an unspoiled tropical paradise perfect for relaxation and marine exploration."]},{"heading":"Pristine White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Feevah boasts some of the most beautiful beaches in Shaviyani Atoll, with soft white sand and crystal-clear waters perfect for swimming and sunbathing."]},{"heading":"World-Class Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef features diverse marine life, colorful corals, and excellent underwater visibility for memorable snorkeling experiences."]},{"heading":"Authentic Island Culture","paragraphs":["Experience genuine Maldivian hospitality and traditional island life in a peaceful setting away from commercial tourism."]},{"heading":"Pristine Sandbank Visits","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks for the ultimate desert island experience with picnics and swimming in crystal-clear waters."]},{"heading":"Beach Highlights","paragraphs":["Feevah''s coastal features include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The island''s underwater world features:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Feevah offers an excellent variety of activities focused on marine adventures, cultural experiences, and natural exploration. The island''s pristine environment provides perfect opportunities for both adventure and relaxation."]},{"heading":"House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the vibrant house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Join local fishermen for authentic fishing experiences using traditional techniques and learn about the island''s fishing heritage."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Tours","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands and discover uninhabited sandbanks, each offering unique experiences and pristine natural beauty."]},{"heading":"Cultural Experiences","paragraphs":["Immerse yourself in local community life, learn traditional crafts, and experience authentic Maldivian customs and traditions."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy magical sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Adventures","paragraphs":["Discover world-class dive sites around Shaviyani Atoll with professional dive operators offering courses and guided dives."]},{"heading":"People & Community Life","paragraphs":["Feevah is home to a close-knit community of approximately 600 residents who have preserved traditional Maldivian ways of life for generations. The island''s population is renowned for their warmth, hospitality, and strong community bonds."]},{"heading":"Daily Life","paragraphs":["Life on Feevah follows the natural rhythms of island living, beginning with morning prayers and continuing with fishing, community work, and family activities. The island maintains a peaceful, unhurried pace that reflects traditional Maldivian values."]},{"heading":"Culture & Traditions","paragraphs":["Feevah preserves authentic Maldivian cultural traditions including traditional music, dance, and handicrafts. The community celebrates Islamic festivals with enthusiasm and maintains traditional practices such as dhoni building and traditional fishing techniques."]},{"heading":"Local Economy","paragraphs":["The island''s economy is primarily based on fishing and increasingly tourism. Many families engage in traditional occupations while also participating in the growing tourism sector through guesthouses, restaurants, and tour services."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Feevah Island?","answer":"Feevah Island can be reached by domestic flight to Funadhoo Airport (1.5 hours from Male) followed by a 30-minute speedboat ride. Seaplane transfers are also available but depend on weather conditions. There''s also a weekly public ferry from Male that takes 16-18 hours."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Feevah?","answer":"The best time to visit Feevah is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable. However, the island can be visited year-round, with the wet season (May-November) offering fewer crowds and lower accommodation rates."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Feevah?","answer":"Feevah offers various budget-friendly and mid-range accommodations including family guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages."},{"question":"What activities are available on Feevah?","answer":"Feevah offers excellent snorkeling from the house reef, diving excursions, island hopping tours, cultural experiences, traditional fishing trips, and sunset cruises. The island is particularly known for its pristine marine environment and authentic cultural experiences."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["foakaidhoo","feydhoo","bileffahi","noomaraa","narudhoo","milandhoo","maroshi","maaungoodhoo","lhaimagu","komandoo","kanditheemu","goidhoo","funadhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'feevah';

